a glimpse inside the self-imposed  confinement and monotony that was Julian  Assange's life for seven years a  security camera shows the rooms inside  the Ecuadorian Embassy where the  WikiLeaks founder languished under  diplomatic protection and therefore  immunity from arrest but mr. Assange is  lawyer has denied claims he upset his  hosts by amongst other things  spreading feces on the walls it's a  difficult situation I've been visiting  him in the embassy for throughout this  seven-year period it's he hasn't had  outside access to the outside he's been  inside a room effectively as you said  for more than seven years people have  children who are six and seven years old  for that for your child's entire life  this man has been inside a room in quite  difficult circumstances now the politics  of the case with respect to Ecuador  change with a change of government with  Linamar Daniel coming to power and ever  since then inside the embassy it's  become more and more difficult to the  point where even Human Rights Watch said  it was akin to solitary confinement  Ecuador allowed British police to haul  away Julian Assange on Thursday he  maintains he stayed not to avoid rape  allegations in Sweden but because he  feared extradition to America for  exposing US government secrets  the Swedish prosecutors came inside the  Ecuadorian embassy to ask him questions  about the case he gave his testimony and  after that they chose to close the  investigation three days after his  arrest and the Ecuador embassy is now  devoid of Assange supporters but his  critics say no charges in Sweden doesn't  amount to exoneration my understanding  is that the only reason the Swedish  authorities have not prosecuted the case  is simply because they couldn't get him  into a Swedish Court it isn't because  they were satisfied that there was no  everything evidence against him there's  no case to answer on the contrary in  fact in Sweden in 2017 the director of  public prosecution in announcing the  rape investigation was being  discontinued suggested mr. Assange is  stay at the Embassy was hindering their  investigation  she said the motive is that there is no  reason to believe that the decision to  surrender him to Sweden can be  executed in the foreseeable future now  mr. Assange is short-term fate is in the  hands of Britain's Home Secretary  if Sweden does press charges as to  whether he would be sent there or to  America he would base his decision on  the seriousness of the allegations which  request was made first whether or not  the person is a fugitive or not and  whether they're simply wanted for trial  or whether they're convicted of an  offense after so long in one room  ultimately Julian Assange probably will  end up abroad for judgment to be passed  on just how his actions will go down in  history Lisa Holland Sky News
 

